3.1 Environment Setup (GitHub Codespaces)
Because Codespaces provides a pre-configured Docker runtime, Minikube runs seamlessly using the Docker driver:

# 1. Install kubectl CLI
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
rm kubectl

# 2. Install Minikube
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube
rm minikube-linux-amd64

# 3. Start cluster with the Docker driver
minikube start --driver=docker

# 4. Verify node health
kubectl get nodes


3.2 Declarative Workload: Deployments & Self-Healing

In production and technical interviews, you rarely create naked Pods (kubectl run) because if a Pod dies, it will not be replaced. Instead, you use a Deployment, which manages a ReplicaSet behind the scenes.

Create a file named nginx-deployment.yaml:

Save as nginx-deployment.yaml:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-app
  labels:
    tier: frontend
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "64Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "128Mi"

Apply and verify:


kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get replicasets
kubectl get pods -l app=web-app


Notice what happened under the hood:

The Controller Manager Loop: kube-controller-manager noticed that the desired count (2 replicas) differed from the current count (0), so it spawned a ReplicaSet to request the two Pods.

The Scheduler & Kubelet: kube-scheduler bound the Pods to minikube, and the local kubelet instructed the container runtime to pull the image and run both replicas.


To observe why Deployments are used in production, simulate a crash by manually deleting one of the pods:

Self-Healing Test:

# Delete one Pod manually:
kubectl delete pod $(kubectl get pods -l app=web-app -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")

# Immediately list pods:
kubectl get pods -l app=web-app

You will see the old pod terminating while the Deployment controller has already launched a brand-new pod with a new name to keep the replica count at 2.


3.3 Decoupling Configuration: ConfigMaps & Secrets
Hardcoding application configuration or credentials directly into container images or manifests violates 12-Factor app architecture. 
Production DevOps workflows use ConfigMaps (plain text) and Secrets (base64-encoded sensitive keys).

Save as app-config.yaml:


apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: web-config
data:
  APP_ENV: "production"
  FEATURE_FLAG: "enabled"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: web-secret
type: Opaque
stringData:
  DB_PASSWORD: "SuperSecretPassword123"


Apply the configuration:

kubectl apply -f app-config.yaml

Update nginx-deployment.yaml to inject the configuration:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-app
  labels:
    tier: frontend
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        envFrom:
        - configMapRef:
            name: web-config
        - secretRef:
            name: web-secret
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "64Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "128Mi"

Apply and verify variable injection:

kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml

POD_NAME=$(kubectl get pods -l app=web-app --field-selector=status.phase=Running -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")
kubectl exec $POD_NAME -- env | grep -E "APP_ENV|FEATURE_FLAG|DB_PASSWORD"


3.4 Service Discovery, Endpoints, & EndpointSlices

Pods have ephemeral, internal IP addresses that change whenever they restart. 
To route traffic reliably across both replicas, create a Service:
Because Pod IP addresses are ephemeral, a Service provides a durable DNS name and virtual IP (ClusterIP).

Save as web-service.yaml:


apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-service
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: web-app
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80


Apply and inspect the discovered IP mappings:

Bash

kubectl apply -f web-service.yaml

# Verify Service creation
kubectl get svc web-service

# Check EndpointSlice (modern replacement for Endpoints)
kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web-service

Access the service locally from Codespaces:

kubectl port-forward service/web-service 8080:80

(Codespaces prompts an "Open in Browser" notification under the Ports view to confirm HTTP 200 from Nginx).

